\title{The Split-Count Law: the Complete Information Content of a
Character-Pinned Fork}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $\chi$ be a surjective homomorphism from the unit group modulo a fixed
modulus $f$ onto an abelian group of order $n\ge 2$, and call a prime $p$
\emph{split} when $\chi(p)=1$. For a semiprime $N=pq$ the labels $\chi(p)$ and
$\chi(q)$ are independent and uniform, while only their product $\chi(N)$ is
observable from the residue of $N$. We determine the complete information such a
\emph{character-pinned fork} carries about the residue class of $N$. Because the
two factors are exchangeable, the sufficient statistic is the split-count
$s=[\chi(p)=1]+[\chi(q)=1]\in\{0,1,2\}$, marginally $\Bin(2,1/n)$; the ordered
pair of split events carries exactly the same information as the count, and the
split event of a single designated factor carries exactly zero. The resulting
channel has the order-universal closed form
\[
\Is(n)=H\bigl(\Bin(2,\tfrac1n)\bigr)-\tfrac1n H\bigl(\tfrac{n-1}{n},0,\tfrac1n\bigr)
-\tfrac{n-1}{n}H\bigl(\tfrac{n-2}{n},\tfrac2n,0\bigr),
\]
depending on the order $n$ alone. We obtain $\Is(2)=1$ bit exactly and
$\Is(3)=\log_2 3-10/9$; a one-bit cap with equality if and only if $n=2$; the
universal comparison $I_\OR(n)\le I_\AND(n)$, strict for $n>2$, via a mirror
principle for binary channels; the failure of the naive hierarchy
$\Is\ge I_\XOR\ge I_\AND\ge I_\OR$ from $n=8$ onwards; the complete asymptotic
law $\Is(n)=\bigl(\log n+2-\tfrac1{2n}+O(n^{-2})\bigr)/(n^2\log 2)$ with every
constant exact; and, for forks of arity $r$, an exact $\chi^2$ divergence
$(n-1)^{1-r}$ yielding the no-amplification law
$I^{(r)}(n)\le (n-1)^{1-r}/\log 2\to 0$ together with the arity constant
$n^rI^{(r)}(n)\log 2-\log n\to r$. In particular the widely quoted ceiling of
$0.3113$ bits for the OR projection of a fork is an artifact of the projection:
the complete channel reaches a full bit. The channel nevertheless remains
factoring-inert.
\end{abstract}

